# PHASE 1: MANDATORY EXISTING PROJECT AUDIT

Before writing or modifying any code, thoroughly inspect the existing ERP project.

Do not start by generating a new application or replacing existing infrastructure.

First identify:

- Existing frontend framework and application architecture.
- Backend framework and API architecture.
- Existing database provider and PostgreSQL schema.
- Supabase project configuration, if applicable.
- Existing authentication implementation.
- User roles and permission structures.
- Product and product category tables.
- Product variants, sizes and packaging definitions.
- Inventory and warehouse modules.
- Stock movement and inventory ledger architecture.
- Sales and POS modules.
- Customer records.
- Existing order-related tables, if available.
- Production and finished goods records.
- Dispatch, delivery and vehicle records, if available.
- Existing notifications and communication integrations.
- Database functions, triggers, RPCs and stored procedures.
- Existing audit logging and transaction handling.
- Existing environment variables and deployment architecture.

Read the relevant source files, database migrations, API endpoints, configuration files and application documentation.

Produce a concise technical audit before implementation.

### Critical integration rules

1. Reuse the existing ERP database wherever technically appropriate.
2. Reuse existing product, category, customer and inventory records.
3. Reuse existing authentication and authorization infrastructure where possible.
4. Reuse existing inventory and sales business logic.
5. Do not create duplicate product or inventory tables without a demonstrated architectural requirement.
6. Do not change existing production, warehouse, POS or financial workflows unnecessarily.
7. Do not delete, overwrite, reset or modify historical ERP records.
8. Do not introduce mock products, mock inventory balances or fabricated business transactions.
9. Never expose database service-role credentials or privileged backend secrets in frontend code.
10. All stock-changing operations must pass through authorised backend business logic.

If the existing ERP has a separate frontend and backend, integrate through authenticated APIs or an appropriate secure service layer.

If the ERP uses Supabase, inspect its existing database schema, Row Level Security policies, Edge Functions, RPC functions and authentication configuration before deciding how the website should connect.

The existing ERP must remain operational throughout development.

